; proc/table.bl — a table that cannot die with the process that asked for it.
;
; ══ THE PROBLEM ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; An ETS table is OWNED by the process that created it, and it is DESTROYED with
; that owner. That is not an accident of the implementation, it is the design: a
; process's tables are part of its memory, and the VM reclaiming them on death is
; what makes ETS safe to use without a cleanup protocol.
;
; It becomes a problem the moment the table is SHARED. A library that creates one
; on demand hands it to whoever asked first:
;
;     (defn get-store []
;       (when (missing? (ets-whereis :store))
;         (ets-new :store)))                 ; ← THE BUG. One line.
;
; The first caller — a request, a test, a scheduler — owns it. It answers, dies
; with the response, and takes the store with it. The next caller finds an empty
; table and starts over. The symptom is never "the table died"; it is a one-shot
; that ran twice, a cache that always misses, a counter that reset, and three
; restarts of confusion before anyone suspects a TABLE.
;
; ══ THE PATTERN ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; `:ets.new` takes a HEIR — `{:heir, Pid, HeirData}`. If the owner dies, the
; table is not destroyed: it is TRANSFERRED to the heir, which receives
;
;     [:ETS-TRANSFER tid from-pid heir-data]
;
; The heir now owns it, and can hand it on with `:ets.give_away/3`. So the
; pattern is a pair of processes, each with one job:
;
;   an OWNER  a trivial process that creates the table and does nothing else —
;             no clauses, no state worth the name — because a process with one
;             job has nothing in it to go wrong
;   an HEIR   a long-lived process that catches tables whose owners died, holds
;             them, and gives them back when a new owner asks
;
; and the sequence on a crash is: **owner dies → heir catches the table → a new
; owner starts → asks the heir → RECLAIMS the table it would otherwise have
; recreated empty.** The table's lifetime is no longer tied to any single
; process, so "who created it" stops being a lifetime question.
;
; ══ WHY NOT JUST SUPERVISE THE OWNER ════════════════════════════════════════
;
; A supervisor restarts the owner, and the restarted owner creates a NEW table —
; empty. Supervision gives you a live process; it does not give you the data
; back. **The heir is the difference between "the owner is running again" and
; "the store is still there."** The two compose: supervise the owner for
; liveness, heir the table for continuity.
;
; ══ USE ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
;     (ns app.cache (:require [proc.table :as table]))
;
;     (table/hold :app-cache {:type :set :options [:public :named_table]})
;
;     (erlang/apply :ets :insert (list :app-cache (tuple :k :v)))  ; plain ETS
;     (erlang/apply :ets :lookup (list :app-cache :k))
;     (table/owner :app-cache)                                     ; who holds it
;
; `hold` is idempotent and costs one `Process/whereis` after the first call. It
; starts the owner and the heir if they are not running, then ASSERTS the
; invariant. Reads and writes stay ordinary ETS calls — this namespace owns
; LIFETIME, never access, which is why it has no get/put of its own.
;
; Three rules make it structural rather than careful, and they are worth
; restating because each one is a hole that was measured once:
;
;   1. ONE CREATOR. `:ets.new` for a held table appears in `table-keeper`'s init
;      and NOWHERE ELSE. `hold` starts a process; it never creates a table, and
;      it has no branch that could. A fallback that "creates it if missing" is
;      the whole bug wearing a safety net.
;   2. AN HEIR, ALWAYS. The heir is named at creation, so there is no window in
;      which the table exists unheired.
;   3. CHECKED. `hold` does not return until THIS table's keeper owns it. An heir
;      holding the table is the designed state BETWEEN two owners — but `hold` is
;      the call that ENDS that window, so a window still open a second later is an
;      error, not a state. That check is not decoration: it is the assertion that
;      caught this file's own bug.
;
; ══ THE FAILURE MODE IS SILENCE, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS A FILE ════════
;
; There are two ways to get the heir wrong and they are both invisible:
;
;   an unquoted `:ETS-TRANSFER`   the clause never matches, so the heir catches
;                                 the table and forgets it
;   the state assoc'd one level   `(assoc (:holding st) …)` rebuilds the INNER
;   too shallow                  map, dropping `:holding` entirely
;
; In both cases the heir still OWNS the table — ownership transfers whether or
; not anything remembers it — so reads keep working, nothing crashes, nothing
; logs, and the store is simply one process away from vanishing instead of two.
; The symptom arrives later and elsewhere: a one-shot that ran twice. Hence rule
; 3, and hence a test that kills the owner and demands the table come back.
;
; ══ SEE ALSO ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; priv/std/proc/sched.bl      — the store, and the first user of this pattern
; priv/lib/datom/store-ets.bl — documents the same hazard ("the table belongs to
;   the creating process and dies with that process") and is the next user
;
; db_connection/holder.ex, under projects/*/*/deps/ — the SAME primitive in
;   production Elixir: a pool is the heir, a checkout process is the owner, and
;   `{:ETS-TRANSFER, holder, pool, {lock, ref, checkin_time}}` is how the table
;   comes home. Same two roles, inverted so the RISKY process holds it.
;
; ══ WHAT IT DOES NOT DO ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; The table is still IN MEMORY. The heir survives a PROCESS crash; nothing here
; survives the VM. Durability across a restart of the node is a different
; question with a different answer (a real store), and this pattern should not
; be mistaken for it. What it guarantees is narrower and exact: **a table's
; lifetime is not accidentally tied to whoever touched it first.**
;
; ══ SEE ALSO

(ns proc.table)

(def HEIR
  "The one heir in the VM. It is deliberately NOT per-table: an heir's whole job
   is to outlive things, and one long-lived process is a better guarantee of that
   than N of them that could each die."
  :bl-table-heir)

(defn missing?
  "True when a registry answered 'nothing there'.

   TWO registries, TWO miss values — `:ets.whereis` answers `:undefined` and
   `Process/whereis` answers `nil` — and comparing the WRONG one is silent: the
   lookup 'succeeds', the branch taken is the other one, and a table ends up
   owned by a process that dies. Public, because every holder needs this
   comparison and a second copy of it is a second chance to get it wrong."
  [v]
  (or (nil? v) (= :undefined v)))

(defn- tid-of [nm] (erlang/apply :ets :whereis (list nm)))

(defn tid
  "The ETS table for `nm`, or nil when it does not exist."
  [nm]
  (let [t (tid-of nm)]
    (when (not (missing? t)) t)))

(defn live? [nm] (some? (tid nm)))

(defn owner
  "The pid that owns `nm`, or nil. Public because it is the invariant, and an
   invariant nobody can read is a comment."
  [nm]
  (let [t (tid-of nm)]
    (when (not (missing? t)) (erlang/apply :ets :info (list t :owner)))))

;; ── the heir ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;
;; Two clauses and a transfer, and NOTHING ELSE it could be asked to do can go
;; wrong — the point of giving a long-lived job to a small process.
;;
;; Its whole state IS its holdings: a flat `{table-name tid}`, no wrapper. That is
;; deliberate, because a nested `{:holding {...}}` has a wrong level to `assoc`
;; into, and getting it wrong is INVISIBLE: the heir still OWNS the table —
;; ownership transfers whether or not the map remembers — so everything looks
;; healthy while every reclaim answers `:nothing` and the table's protection
;; quietly drops from two-deep to one-deep. One level: nothing to get wrong.
;;
;; The name it files a table under is the ONE WE PUT IN THE TRANSFER (`HeirData`),
;; not something it works out afterwards. Two reasons, and the second is the one
;; that matters: an `:ets.info(tid, :name)` lookup is a second chance to disagree
;; about identity, and it does not work AT ALL for an UNNAMED table — the kind
;; `datom/store-ets` makes, which is the next user of this pattern.
;;
;; `:"ETS-TRANSFER"` is QUOTED. Unquoted, `:ETS-TRANSFER` is not a name, it is the
;; expression `:ETS - TRANSFER`: it compiles to something, and it never matches,
;; so the clause is dead code that reads as live. Both failures produce the same
;; symptom — a table that is alive, owned, and no longer recoverable.

(defserver table-heir
  (init [_] (ok {}))
  (handle-info [:"ETS-TRANSFER" t _from nm] [st]
    (noreply (assoc st nm t)))
  (handle-call [:give-to nm pid] [_from st]
    (let [t (get st nm)]
      (if (some? t)
        (do (erlang/apply :ets :give_away (list t pid nil))
            (reply :given (assoc st nm nil)))
        (reply :nothing st)))))

;; ── the owner ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

(defn- keeper-name [nm] (keyword (str (name nm) "-keeper")))

(defn- make-table [nm spec]
  (let [type (:type spec :set)
        base (or (:options spec) [:public :named_table])
        heir (Process/whereis HEIR)
        opts (concat [type] base (if (some? heir) [(tuple :heir heir nm)] []))]
    (erlang/apply :ets :new (list nm (to-list opts)))))

(defserver table-keeper
  (init [spec]
    (do
      ;; RECLAIM before creating: a keeper that starts after its predecessor
      ;; died must resume the table the heir is holding, not make a fresh one.
      ;; This is the line that turns a crash from a data loss into a hiccup.
      (when (some? (Process/whereis HEIR))
        (call HEIR [:give-to (:table spec) (erlang/self)]))
      (when (missing? (tid-of (:table spec)))
        (make-table (:table spec) spec))
      (ok {:table (:table spec)}))))

(defn- claim!
  "Wait — bounded — for `nm` to be owned by `keeper`.

   The keeper reclaims and creates during its init, so ownership lands a moment
   after `start` returns; and when two callers race to start it, the loser has
   seen a name that is registered but not yet finished initialising. Neither is a
   failure, so neither may be reported as one.

   But a TIMEOUT is: it means the table is alive and the keeper does not hold it,
   which is precisely the silent state this file exists to abolish — a store that
   answers reads and quietly has no protector. Loud, here, beats mysterious
   later."
  [nm keeper]
  (loop [n 0]
    (cond
      (= (owner nm) keeper) nm
      (> n 200)
      (throw (ex-info (str "proc.table: " nm " has no keeper after 1s — owner is "
                           (owner nm) ", wanted " keeper
                           ". The table is alive; its protection is not.")
                      {:table nm :owner (owner nm) :keeper keeper}))
      :else
      (do
        (Process/sleep 5)
        (recur (+ n 1))))))

(defn- start-once!
  "Start `srv` under `nm` unless it is already there. Losing a start race is not
   a failure — the winner's process is the one we wanted."
  [srv arg nm]
  (when (missing? (Process/whereis nm))
    (try
      (start srv arg {:name nm})
      (catch e
        (when (missing? (Process/whereis nm))
          (throw (ex-info (str "proc.table: " nm " would not start: "
                               (if (map? e) (get e :message) (str e)))
                          {:error e})))))))

(defn hold
  "Ensure `nm` exists, is owned by a process that can outlive its users, and has
   an heir. Returns `nm`; the table itself is ordinary ETS.

   `spec` is `{:type :set | :ordered_set | :bag, :options [...]}` — anything
   `:ets.new` accepts, minus the type (which has its own key) and minus `:heir`
   (which is this namespace's job, and is appended for you).

   Idempotent, and cheap after the first call: one `Process/whereis` per role,
   then the assertion. Called from the first user, because a store that has to
   be started by hand is a store that is sometimes not started."
  ([nm] (hold nm {}))
  ([nm spec]
   ;; the HEIR first: the owner names it as the table's heir AT CREATION, so a
   ;; table must never be born into a VM with no heir to catch it
   (start-once! table-heir nil HEIR)
   (let [keeper (keeper-name nm)]
     (start-once! table-keeper (assoc spec :table nm) keeper)
     ;; and it must be the KEEPER that holds it. An heir holding the table is the
     ;; designed state between two owners — but `hold` is the call that ENDS that
     ;; window, so leaving it open is the failure, not the state.
     (claim! nm (Process/whereis keeper))
     nm)))
